import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
---
<Base
  title="How a photo gets on screen — LightPhotos Blog"
  desc="To open one photo, the app does one, two or three decodes, and the file format does not decide how many. This post follows the whole trip from a file on disk to pixels on your monitor."
  active="blogs"
>
  <main class="lp-page">
    <article class="lp-article">
      <header class="lp-article-head">
        <p class="lp-article-meta"><a href="/blogs.html">Blogs</a> &middot; <time datetime="2026-07-08">July 8, 2026</time></p>
        <h1>How a photo gets on screen</h1>
        <p class="lp-article-dek">
          To open one photo, the app does one, two or three decodes, and the
          file format does not decide how many. This post follows the whole
          trip from a file on disk to pixels on your monitor.
        </p>
        <p class="lp-article-byline">Andrew Yao &middot; 5 min read</p>
      </header>

      <aside class="lp-tldr">
        <p class="lp-tldr-label">TL;DR</p>
        <p>
          When you open a photo, LightPhotos first decodes it (turns the file
          into pixels) in the fastest way it can. If the result is as big as
          the window needs, it stops there. If not, it runs a slower, correct
          decode next. It runs a third decode only if you zoom in past the
          detail that is already on screen. After that, zooming, panning and
          every slider are only math on the graphics card, and the app does not
          touch the image data again.
        </p>
      </aside>

      <h2>What I learned</h2>
      <ul class="lp-article-list">
        <li>
          <strong>Show something cheap while the real work runs.</strong> When
          the app showed the preview from the camera first, a 250 ms wait
          became a 35 ms wait. But nothing actually got faster.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Base the next step on what came back.</strong> I first
          checked "is this a RAW file?" That seemed sensible, but it would have
          led to a growing list of special cases for each format. The check "is
          the result big enough?" is one rule that covers every format.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Send the photo to the graphics card once.</strong> Anything
          that happens 60 times a second, like a zoom or a slider drag, must
          change only a few numbers. It must never redo the expensive decode.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Keep platform differences in one place.</strong> The four
          builds differ only in how they read files. Everything after that is
          shared, so the platforms cannot drift apart.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Three different jobs</h2>
      <p>
        I want LightPhotos to feel instant. You open a folder of holiday
        photos, and the thumbnails fill in. You double-click one, and it opens
        at full size. You turn the scroll wheel, and it zooms. None of that
        should surprise anyone, and that is my goal.
      </p>
      <p>
        A photo takes one of three paths through the app. I treat each path as
        its own problem.
      </p>
      <ul class="lp-article-list">
        <li>
          <strong>Opening.</strong> You clicked, and you want the photo large on
          screen right now.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Browsing.</strong> The grid needs a small version of every
          photo that you can see at the moment.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Exporting.</strong> You want a JPEG on disk with your edits
          applied.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        When you open a photo, the important thing is the time between your
        click and the first image on screen. Browsing means doing one small job
        a hundred times without slowing down. An export must get the pixels
        exactly right. It can take as long as it needs, because you already
        pressed the button and walked away.
      </p>

      <h2>Opening a photo</h2>
      <p>
        A camera RAW file is not really an image yet. It is the raw readout
        from the sensor, and it takes real work to turn it into something you
        can look at. That step is called demosaicing. On my machine, it takes
        approximately 250 ms for a Sony ARW file at screen size.
      </p>
      <p>
        But almost every RAW file comes with a shortcut. Your camera already
        saved a small JPEG preview inside the file, so that it could show you
        the shot on its own screen. On that Sony, the preview is 1616 pixels on
        the long side. It takes approximately 35 ms to extract.
      </p>
      <p>
        So the first pass always does the same thing. It gets the cheapest
        decode it can near the size that I asked for. If the file has an
        embedded preview that is good enough, it uses that preview. Then the
        app checks one thing.
      </p>
      <blockquote class="lp-pullquote">
        Is the result as big as the window actually needs?
      </blockquote>
      <p>
        If it is, the app stops there, and you see the final result after one
        decode. That happens with every JPEG. A JPEG has no embedded preview,
        so the first pass decodes it at the requested size. It also happens
        with a RAW file in a smaller window. If the loupe (the single-photo
        view) needs only 1400 pixels, the 1616-pixel camera preview is already
        bigger than that. So the expensive decode never runs.
      </p>
      <p>
        If the result is too small, the app queues a second decode at the full
        target size. When that decode is done, the app swaps it in. That is the
        case I see most days. My window asks for approximately 2560 pixels, and
        the camera preview is 1616.
      </p>

      <figure class="lp-figure">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 640 220" role="img" aria-label="Three cases. A JPEG, or a RAW with a preview as big as the window, needs one decode and is done. A RAW with a preview smaller than the window needs two: a 35 millisecond first pass on screen, then a 250 millisecond second pass that swaps in. If you zoom past the screen-size version, a third full-resolution decode runs on demand. The rule is the size of the result, not the file format.">
          <text class="lp-fig-label" x="0" y="12">HOW MANY DECODES ONE PHOTO NEEDS</text>

          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="0" y="34">JPEG, or a RAW with a preview as big as the window</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box-accent" x="0" y="42" width="180" height="26" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="90" y="59" text-anchor="middle">Pass 1</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="192" y="59">one decode, done</text>

          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="0" y="96">RAW with a preview smaller than the window</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="0" y="104" width="180" height="26" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="90" y="121" text-anchor="middle">Pass 1 &middot; 35 ms</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box-accent" x="188" y="104" width="240" height="26" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="308" y="121" text-anchor="middle">Pass 2 &middot; 250 ms</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="440" y="121">two decodes</text>

          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="0" y="158">You zoom past the screen-size version</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="0" y="166" width="180" height="26" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="90" y="183" text-anchor="middle">Pass 1</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="188" y="166" width="240" height="26" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="308" y="183" text-anchor="middle">Pass 2</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box-accent" x="436" y="166" width="172" height="26" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="522" y="183" text-anchor="middle">Pass 3 &middot; on demand</text>

          <text class="lp-fig-s lp-fig-bad" x="0" y="212">The rule is the size of the result, not the file format.</text>
        </svg>
        <figcaption>
          Nothing here got faster. The app shows something useful while the
          expensive work still runs. And it skips that work when it is not
          needed.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p>
        I like this rule much better than my first rule, which checked whether
        the file was a RAW. A check of the result size is one rule that covers
        every format. That includes formats I have not added yet.
      </p>
      <p>
        The third pass is for zooming. A full-resolution decode takes seconds
        and hundreds of megabytes of memory. So it runs only when you zoom in
        past the point where the screen-size version still has enough detail.
      </p>
      <p>
        I am pleased with one more detail. When you use the arrow keys to move
        through a shoot, the app loads the photos on each side of you ahead of
        time. But it uses only the cheap first pass for them. A full decode for
        a photo you have not looked at yet would compete with the photo you
        are looking at now. When you move onto that photo, it gets the full
        decode like any other photo.
      </p>

      <h2>After that, it all happens on the graphics card</h2>
      <p>
        After the app decodes a photo, it sends the photo to the graphics card
        (the GPU) once. Zooming and panning never touch the image data again.
        They only change a few numbers that the app sends to the card each
        frame.
      </p>
      <p>
        The editing sliders work the same way. When you drag Exposure, the new
        value goes to the GPU. A shader, which is a small program that runs on
        the card, then redraws the picture immediately. Nothing gets decoded
        again, and the main processor never loops over the pixels. You see the
        original decode with some math applied on its way to the screen.
      </p>

      <h2>Two ways of drawing</h2>
      <p>
        egui draws the buttons, grid, filmstrip and sliders. egui is a Rust
        interface library that redraws the whole interface every frame. A few
        hundred lines of hand-written GPU code draw the photo itself,
        underneath a transparent panel.
      </p>
      <p>
        This split lets me write the interface in the loose, convenient style
        that egui encourages. It also keeps the pixel-heavy part small enough
        to tune by hand. With a general-purpose toolkit, the photo would be a
        widget. It is hard to make a widget take a 24-megapixel image with a
        custom shader.
      </p>

      <h2>Where the four builds meet</h2>
      <p>
        The macOS, Linux, Windows and browser builds differ in only one place:
        how they turn a file into pixels. After that, they all use the same
        code.
      </p>

      <figure class="lp-figure">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 640 280" role="img" aria-label="Diagram: four platform builds split only at the decode layer. Then they all use one shared edit pipeline, which feeds both the GPU tone shader and the exporter.">
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="0" y="0" width="140" height="30" rx="6"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="70" y="20" text-anchor="middle">macOS</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="160" y="0" width="140" height="30" rx="6"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="230" y="20" text-anchor="middle">Linux</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="320" y="0" width="140" height="30" rx="6"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="390" y="20" text-anchor="middle">Windows</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="480" y="0" width="140" height="30" rx="6"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="550" y="20" text-anchor="middle">Browser</text>

          <path class="lp-fig-line" d="M70 30 V52 M230 30 V52 M390 30 V52 M550 30 V52"></path>

          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="0" y="52" width="140" height="34" rx="6"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="70" y="73" text-anchor="middle">ImageIO</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="160" y="52" width="300" height="34" rx="6"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="310" y="73" text-anchor="middle">Rust decoders</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="480" y="52" width="140" height="34" rx="6"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="550" y="73" text-anchor="middle">same, in Web Workers</text>

          <path class="lp-fig-line" d="M70 86 V100 H550 M550 86 V100 M310 86 V152"></path>
          <line class="lp-fig-line-soft" x1="0" y1="116" x2="640" y2="116" stroke-dasharray="3 4"></line>
          <text class="lp-fig-label" x="0" y="136">THE ONLY FORK. EVERYTHING BELOW IS SHARED</text>
          <path class="lp-fig-arrow" d="M304 152 L316 152 L310 162 Z"></path>

          <rect class="lp-fig-box-accent" x="90" y="166" width="460" height="34" rx="6"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="320" y="187" text-anchor="middle">One edit function, in linear light</text>

          <path class="lp-fig-line" d="M200 200 V216 H440 M440 200 V216 M200 216 V230 M440 216 V230"></path>
          <path class="lp-fig-arrow" d="M194 230 L206 230 L200 240 Z M434 230 L446 230 L440 240 Z"></path>

          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="90" y="244" width="220" height="34" rx="6"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="200" y="265" text-anchor="middle">Tone shader on the GPU</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="330" y="244" width="220" height="34" rx="6"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="440" y="265" text-anchor="middle">Exported JPEG</text>
        </svg>
        <figcaption>
          The platforms split only at the decode step. Everything after that is
          shared, so the platforms cannot drift apart.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p>
        The GPU tone shader is a direct translation of that one edit function.
        The function that bakes your edits into finished pixels has exactly two
        users: the exporter and the thumbnail you are looking at.
      </p>
      <p>
        That is why an exported JPEG matches what you saw on screen. I never
        built that as a separate feature. It comes from having only one code
        path. I would much rather get that for free than keep it true by hand.
      </p>

      <p>
        If you want to see how this feels, the
        <a href="/downloads.html">downloads page</a> has builds for all four
        platforms.
      </p>

